---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { photos, scenes } from '../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../components/CTABand.astro';
import YouTubeFacade from '../components/YouTubeFacade.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';

const crumbs = [{ label: 'About Mo', href: '/about/' }];
const welcomeId = site.integrations.welcomeVideoId;
const welcomePoster = site.integrations.welcomeVideoPoster;
const welcomeUploaded = site.integrations.welcomeVideoUploadDate;
const videoSchema =
  welcomeId && welcomeUploaded
    ? [
        {
          '@context': 'https://schema.org',
          '@type': 'VideoObject',
          name: 'Meet Mo Lumen: a short introduction',
          description:
            'A short face-to-camera introduction from astrologer Maureen "Mo" Lumen, on how she works and what a reading with her is like.',
          uploadDate: welcomeUploaded,
          embedUrl: `https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/${welcomeId}`,
          contentUrl: `https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=${welcomeId}`,
          ...(welcomePoster ? { thumbnailUrl: new URL(welcomePoster, site.url).href } : {}),
          publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: site.name, url: site.url },
        },
      ]
    : [];
const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Person',
    name: 'Maureen "Mo" Lumen',
    jobTitle: 'Astrologer',
    url: `${site.url}/about/`,
    worksFor: { '@type': 'ProfessionalService', name: site.name, url: site.url },
    memberOf: [
      { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'National Council for Geocosmic Research (NCGR)' },
      { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Organization for Professional Astrologers (OPA)' },
      { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'International Society for Astrological Research (ISAR)' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Mo: astrologer, teacher, writer"
  description="Meet Maureen 'Mo' Lumen: astrologer, teacher, writer, and lifelong observer of patterns, cycles, and the ways the pieces of a life fit together."
  path="/about/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  schema={[...schema, ...videoSchema]}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />

  <section class="hero hero--split about-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1>Hello! I'm Maureen … a.k.a. Mo</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Astrologer. Teacher. Writer. Lifelong observer of patterns, cycles, people, and the moment
          when separate pieces suddenly reveal a larger whole.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.aboutMo.src}
        srcset={scenes.aboutMo.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.aboutMo.alt}
        width={scenes.aboutMo.width}
        height={scenes.aboutMo.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  {welcomeId && (
    <section class="section">
      <div class="container" style="max-width: 46rem;">
        <p class="eyebrow">A minute with Mo</p>
        <h2>Before you decide, hear how I actually talk</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          A reading is a conversation with a real person, not a performance. Here is a minute with
          me before we ever sit down together.
        </p>
        <div style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
          <YouTubeFacade
            videoId={welcomeId}
            title="Meet Mo Lumen: a short introduction"
            poster={welcomePoster || undefined}
            context="about-welcome"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container grid grid--2" style="align-items: start;">
      <div class="prose flow">
        <p class="eyebrow">The long curiosity</p>
        <h2>I have been following this question for a long time</h2>
        <p>
          Astrology has fascinated me since my age was in the single digits. I followed it for
          years before I ever imagined making it my profession. Alongside that curiosity grew
          another one: living systems, cycles within cycles, and the relationships that make a
          whole more than a collection of separate parts.
        </p>
        <p>
          As a science teacher, I loved the moment when separate facts stopped being separate: one
          cycle connected to another, one system affected another, and the larger pattern came into
          view. The closer I looked at living systems, the more extraordinary they became.
        </p>
        <p>
          Astrology keeps me in that same state of curiosity, not because science proves astrology.
          It does not. Teaching science trained me to observe carefully, ask better questions,
          explain complicated ideas, and respect how much complexity can live inside one system.
          Those habits come with me when I look at a chart.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="portrait-card">
        <img
          src={photos.moAbout.src}
          alt={photos.moAbout.alt}
          width={photos.moAbout.width}
          height={photos.moAbout.height}
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Mo Lumen</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <p class="eyebrow">How that became a practice</p>
      <h2>I study widely because charts are complicated</h2>
      <p>
        My training has crossed traditional, horary, modern, Uranian, dwarf-planet, and other
        contemporary approaches. I value technique, but I am less interested in forcing every
        chart through one school than in understanding what the chart in front of me is saying as
        a whole.
      </p>
      <p>
        No two people live the same symbolism in exactly the same way. I may see several ways a
        pattern could be expressing itself; the person living the chart brings the part I cannot
        know from technique alone. That is why I think of a reading as synthesis and conversation,
        not a performance in which I announce who you are.
      </p>
      <blockquote>There is fate and there is choice, and a good reading helps you tell the difference.</blockquote>
      <p>
        I want you to understand what I am seeing and why I am seeing it. I will tell you what I
        notice, ask when I need to understand how something is showing up in your life, and leave
        the decisions where they belong: with you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container grid grid--2" style="align-items: start;">
      <div class="flow">
        <p class="eyebrow">Away from the chart</p>
        <h2>The things that keep me oriented</h2>
        <p>
          My family is the center of my life. I am drawn to spiritual practice, nature, travel,
          learning, and especially the ocean. These are the places and practices that remind me I
          am part of something larger and that there is always another way of seeing.
        </p>
        <p>
          That sense of wonder matters to me. I do not want rigor to flatten it, and I do not want
          wonder to become an excuse for certainty. I would rather stay curious enough to keep
          looking.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="card">
        <h2 style="font-size: var(--text-xl); margin: 0;">Want the formal background?</h2>
        <p>
          My certifications, professional memberships, teaching roles, and ethics commitments live
          on a separate page so this one can stay about the person behind the practice.
        </p>
        <p style="margin: 0;"><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/credentials/">See Training & Credentials</a></p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Want to know what it feels like to work together?"
    body="See how I prepare, how the conversation unfolds, and where your own experience enters the interpretation before you decide whether to book."
    buttonLabel="How Readings Work"
    buttonHref="/how-readings-work/"
    secondaryLabel="See How I Read a Chart"
    secondaryHref="/approach/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
